Benchmark contamination in Large Language Models (LLMs) undermines evaluation validity, yet most detection methods require privileged access to training data, model internals, or output probabilities.
We present the first systematic characterization of IRT person-fit statistics for contamination detection under black-box access: the framework requires only binary response patterns from the target model---no access to weights, gradients, logits, or training data---combined with externally calibrated 4PL item parameters from a large-scale item bank; diagnostic $\theta$-anchoring additionally requires a clean baseline.
A contaminated LLM produces aberrant response patterns analogous to a test-taker with item preknowledge.
Using $\ell_z^*$ in a difference-in-differences design ($\Delta\ell_z^*$) across three 7--8B model families ($n{=}10$ seeds each) on MMLU (14,042 items) and ARC-Challenge (295 items), we identify four behavior boundaries:
(1) an empirical inverted-U dose-response in one model family, where detection peaks at 25\% contamination ($|d|{=}8.22$) and declines at 50\% ($|d|{=}1.90$) due to $\theta$-shift absorption;
(2) model-specific $\theta$-shift dynamics linking baseline ability to detection sensitivity;
(3) a detection-diagnosis tradeoff where simpler statistics yield stronger detection but lack $\ell_z^*$'s diagnostic capabilities (seen/unseen decomposition, mechanistic $\theta$-shift analysis);
and (4) a preliminary post-RL observation ($N{=}1$) that GRPO erases the accuracy gap while $\ell_z^*$ response-pattern structure persists.
These findings establish when and why binary response patterns carry contamination information beyond aggregate accuracy.

